266:
(a) The judge of the municipal court may, by written order establish a schedule of bails and unless otherwise ordered by the judge of the court, an accused shall be released from custody upon posting bail as fixed in the schedule.
(b) As soon as is reasonably practicable following any arrest, but no later than 48 hours if the arrest was without a warrant, or 72 hours following an arrest with a warrant, unless the accused has made bond in the meantime, the arresting officer or other law enforcement officer having custody of the accused shall present the accused before a municipal judge or other judicial officer for first appearance. First appearance hearings shall be conducted in the manner prescribed by law and the Uniform Rules of Municipal Courts and bail shall be determined in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 17-6-1.
(c) The arresting officer, chief of police, a municipal judge, or any other officer designated by the chief of police may accept bond of the accused payable to the city conditioned upon the appearance of the accused at the next session or any subsequent session of the municipal court as stated in the bond, to answer the charges. Secured bonds for the appearance of persons charged with an offense may be cash, personal, or real property.
(d) The bond shall be in writing in an amount set forth in an established bail schedule or bail order of the court.
(e) In lieu of an appearance bond, and notwithstanding a bail schedule created by order of the chief judge of the municipal court, a person arrested for a misdemeanor offense or ordinance violation may be released on his own recognizance or an unsecured bond if a judge, the chief of police, or the arresting officer makes a determination that the person arrested will appear at the time and day set for the court appearance in view of the following considerations:
(1) The seriousness of the offense charged;
(2) The penalty provided by law;
(3) The number of charges pending against the defendant;
(4) The criminal record of the defendant;
(5) The residence of the defendant and the length of time of residence;
(6) The defendant's age;
(7) The defendant's marital status and number of children;
(8) Residency of the relatives of the defendant;
(9) Employment of the defendant (by whom, nature of work and how long employed);
(10) Former employment of the defendant;
(11) Amount of earnings and financial resources of the defendant;
(12) The defendant's character, reputation and previous criminal history;
(13) The defendant's mental condition;
(14) Any medical needs of the defendant;
(15) Any records indicating the defendant has a recent history or pattern of failing to appear for required court appearances;
(16) Whether the defendant has membership in any clubs or societies, church affiliations and other things which demonstrate that the defendant is a responsible member of the community with established community ties.
(17) Any release of a defendant by a police officer prior to a first appearance hearing must be approved by a supervisor or the chief of police.
(f) Any person released under section (e) above shall if released on the person's own recognizance, sign and receive a copy of the citation describing the violation for which the person was arrested.
(g) Any person released under section (e) above shall, if released on an unsecured bond, sign and receive a copy of the appearance bond in an amount set forth in an established bail schedule or bail order of the court. Upon the failure to appear of any person who has been released from custody on an unsecured bond that later fails to appear in court as required, the principle and any surety to the unsecured bond shall be assessed for the full amount set forth in the bond. In addition, the defendant will be guilty of the offense of contempt of court and a bench warrant shall be issued for their arrest.
(h) Pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 17-6-11, any person cited for a traffic offense shall be released in lieu of bail upon showing his or her driver's license, however no person shall be released on citation if they do not provide a signature as required in O.C.G.A. § 40-13-2.1.
(i) Pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 17-4-23, a person accused of any ordinance enacted by local law or any misdemeanor violation of O.C.G.A. § 16-7-21, 16-8-14, 16-8-14.1, or 16-13-30 which is to be tried in the municipal court may be arrested by the issuance of a citation.
(j) All persons arrested or notified by citation or ticket of parking violations shall be released on their own recognizance.
(Ord. No. 2019-0001 , § 1, 3-5-2019)
